#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECURITY CONTROL. The text here is deliberately plain English, not the
# Simplified Technical English the rest of this repository uses. A vague
# comment on a security control gets deleted by the next person who tidies up.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# This rule gives the appliance account ONE input device, by owner. That is
# what lets the account stay out of group "input". Do not replace OWNER= with
# GROUP="input", and do not put the service account in group "input".
#
# What group "input" would grant, and why you must not use it: read access to
# every input device on this machine, including devices that do not exist yet.
# On Debian every /dev/input/event* node is already 0660 root:input, so any
# member of that group can read the touchscreen, and every keystroke from any
# USB keyboard somebody plugs in later, including a password typed at the
# console. This appliance stands in a public place and already records what
# people say. A process that can also read every key is a keylogger, and
# nothing in this software needs one. Widen this rule and the appliance
# becomes one, with nothing on the display to show it.
#
# The match below is pinned to the kernel's device topology on purpose, not to
# the device name. A USB device supplies its own name string, so anyone with
# thirty seconds of physical access could plug in a gadget that calls itself
# "recorder-buttons". Nothing plugged into a USB port can claim to be a
# platform device named recorder_keys bound to the gpio-keys driver.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# CAUTION: do not add ATTRS{name}=="recorder-buttons" to the rule below. It
# looks like one more test, and it makes the full rule agree with no device.
#
# udev examines the parents of a device for SUBSYSTEMS, DRIVERS, KERNELS and
# ATTRS, and all of these keys in one rule must agree on the same parent. The
# name is a property of the inputN device, but the driver and the name of the
# node are properties of its parent.
#
# Thus no parent can agree with the two keys together. The rule agrees with no
# device, and udev gives no error. See section 8.5 of deploy/README.md.
#
# KERNELS is the name of the node in deploy/recorder-keys-overlay.dts. If you
# give that node a different name, change this value also. deploy-pi.sh puts
# the account of the systemd unit in the position of {{USER}}.

SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event*", SUBSYSTEMS=="platform", DRIVERS=="gpio-keys", KERNELS=="recorder_keys", SYMLINK+="input/recorder-buttons", OWNER="{{USER}}", GROUP="root", MODE="0400"

# The touchscreen of the display module. Avalonia reads it through libinput,
# which needs the account to be able to open the device node.
#
# This rule is the second half of keeping the account out of group "input".
# Without it, the only way the appliance can see the touchscreen is to be in
# that group, which is the thing the comment above tells you not to do. With
# it, the account owns exactly two input devices: the buttons and the panel it
# is drawing on.
#
# The match is pinned to the driver and not to the name for the same reason as
# the rule above. Nothing plugged into a USB port can claim to be an i2c device
# bound to the Goodix-TS driver.
#
# Unlike the rule above, ATTRS{name} is safe to include here. udev requires
# every parent-matching key in one rule to match the same ancestor, and on this
# device SUBSYSTEMS, DRIVERS and ATTRS{name} all live on the i2c parent
# (11-005d). Verified with "udevadm info -a -p /sys/class/input/event10".

SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event*", SUBSYSTEMS=="i2c", DRIVERS=="Goodix-TS", ATTRS{name}=="gt911", SYMLINK+="input/appliance-touchscreen", OWNER="{{USER}}", GROUP="root", MODE="0400"
